course: everyday
lesson: 287
title: Caring for pets and staying safe around farm, wild and zoo animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pet is not a toy: it depends on you for food, shade, calm company and fair play every single day, even when you are tired of looking after it.
explain: Some foods that are fine for people are poisonous for pets: onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes and raisins, salty food and the sweetener xylitol in some sweets and gum. That is why pets get their own food, not leftovers from your plate.
explain: Wild, farm and zoo animals are not pets. A rooster may attack to guard his hens, and a bear may chase someone who runs, so we stay calm, move away slowly and keep behind barriers.
explain: Example: a boy in Montana sees a bear at the edge of the trail. He does not run. He stays next to his dad, they talk in calm voices and slowly back away the way they came, giving the bear plenty of room.
explain: Some fears are bigger than the danger: sharks very rarely attack people, because we are not their normal food. A real snake bite, though, is always an emergency.
fact: Pets should eat their own pet food, not leftovers from people's plates.
fact: Onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, very salty food and the sweetener xylitol can make pets very ill.
fact: A new baby or a new pet should be introduced slowly, always with an adult watching, because animals can feel jealous or scared.
fact: A pet is a responsibility for its whole life; if a family truly cannot keep it, they find it a new home through a shelter.
fact: Abandoning a pet is cruel and against the law.
fact: In play, let a pet catch the toy sometimes; play that always ends in frustration makes animals stressed or snappy.
fact: Cages and hutches must be kept in the shade, because small animals can overheat and die quickly in the sun.
fact: If a rooster chases you, walk away calmly and tell its owner; some roosters attack to protect their hens.
fact: After any snake bite, call the emergency number, stay calm and keep still; never suck the bite or cut it.
fact: If you see a bear, stay with adults, do not run, speak calmly and back away slowly, because running can make a bear chase you.
fact: At the zoo, follow the signs, stay behind the barriers and never climb fences; zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous.
fact: Sharks rarely attack people, because people are not their normal food.
exercise: Your dog begs at the table while you eat. What should you do? | Give the dog its own dog food at its mealtime | Share small bits of your dinner with it, so it feels part of the family | Give it the leftover onions and gravy | Give it a little chocolate as a treat
exercise: Which of these foods can make a dog or cat very ill? | Grapes and raisins | Plain cooked chicken | Its own pet food from the shop | Fresh clean water in a bowl
exercise: Why should pets not eat sweets or gum with xylitol in them? | Xylitol is a sweetener that can poison pets | It only makes their teeth a bit sticky for a day or so | It makes their fur grow too fast | It is safe for pets in any amount
exercise: Your family brings a new kitten home and you already have a dog. What is the best way to start? | Introduce them slowly with an adult watching | Put them in a room alone together to sort it out | Keep the dog shut away from the family forever | Let the dog sniff the kitten without any adult around
exercise: Why should a new baby and the family dog meet slowly, with an adult there? | The dog may feel jealous or scared of the newcomer | Dogs never notice a new baby at all, so it doesn't matter how | Babies are frightened of every animal | It is only a tradition with no reason
exercise: A boy is bored of feeding his rabbit and wants to let it go in the park. What is right? | Keep caring for it, or find it a new home through a shelter | Let it go, because rabbits love living wild | Leave it outside a shop in a box for someone kind to take home | Stop feeding it until his parents notice
exercise: Why is it wrong to dump a pet you no longer want? | It is cruel, and it is against the law | Pets always find their way home anyway | It is fine if the pet is small | Only dogs are protected by the law
exercise: You play tug with a puppy and never let it win. What may happen? | The puppy gets frustrated and may become snappy | The puppy learns to be calmer and calmer every time | The puppy stops wanting to play forever, happily | Nothing, because puppies do not feel frustration
exercise: It is a hot sunny afternoon and the guinea pig's hutch is in full sun. What should you do? | Move the hutch into the shade straight away | Leave it, because guinea pigs love heat | Cover the hutch with a dark plastic sheet to keep the light out | Wait until evening and then check on it
exercise: A rooster runs at you on your uncle's farm. What should you do? | Walk away calmly and tell the owner | Chase it back across the yard to show it who is boss | Pick it up to calm it down | Kick at it so it learns a lesson
exercise: Why might a rooster attack a person? | He may be trying to protect his hens | Roosters attack only when they are hungry | He thinks the person is another rooster's egg | Roosters never attack anyone at all, ever
exercise: A snake bites your friend on a walk. What should you do? | Call the emergency number and keep your friend calm and still | Suck out the poison with your mouth | Cut the bite so the poison bleeds out | Make your friend run home as fast as possible to get help there
exercise: A family hiking in Canada sees a bear. What should the children do? | Stay with the adults, speak calmly and back away slowly | Run away as fast as they can | Throw food at the bear to make friends with it so it goes away | Walk closer to take a photo
exercise: Why should you not run from a bear? | Running can make the bear chase you | Bears are frightened of fast movement | Bears cannot run fast at all on flat ground | Running makes you invisible to the bear
exercise: At the zoo you want a closer look at the tigers. What is right? | Stay behind the barrier and follow the signs | Climb the fence a little to see better, just for a moment | Reach through the bars to touch one | Throw something to wake them up
exercise: Do sharks usually want to eat people? | No, shark attacks are rare because people are not their normal food | Yes, sharks hunt people whenever they can and follow boats about | Yes, people are sharks' favourite food | Only on Sundays when the beach is busy
test: A girl in Leeds wants to give her cat the leftover garlic bread from dinner. What should she do instead? | Give the cat its own cat food | Give it only a small piece of the bread | Scrape off the garlic and give it the rest | Mix the bread into the cat's water
test: Which of these is true? | Chocolate is dangerous for dogs | Chocolate is good for a dog's heart | Dogs need sweets to stay happy and healthy | A little salt in every meal is good for pets
test: Grandma's old dog growls when the family's new puppy arrives. What is the best thing to do? | Let them get used to each other slowly with an adult watching | Shut them in a cupboard together | Shout at the old dog until it stops growling and leaves the puppy | Give the old dog away at once
test: A family is moving to a flat where pets are not allowed. What is the responsible choice? | Find the pet a new home through a shelter | Leave the pet behind in the empty house | Let it loose in the countryside | Leave it at the old address for the new owners to find
test: Why is it kind to let your cat catch the feather toy sometimes? | Winning sometimes keeps play fun and not stressful | Cats do not care at all whether they catch it or not | It teaches the cat to stop playing | It makes the cat hungry for toys
test: A boy in Phoenix, Arizona, puts his hamster's cage on a sunny windowsill. What could happen? | The hamster could overheat and die quickly | The hamster will just get a nice tan | The hamster will sleep better and more deeply in the warm sun | Nothing, because cages keep out heat
test: A boy is bitten by a snake in Texas. His friend says, "Cut it and suck the poison out!" Is the friend right? | No: call the emergency number, keep still and do not cut or suck | Yes, that is the fastest way to help | Yes, but only if the snake was small | No, he should run to a doctor on his own without telling anyone at all
test: Campers in Colorado meet a bear on the path. Which plan is safest? | Keep together, talk calmly and slowly back away | Scatter in all directions and run, so the bear can't choose who to chase | Climb onto the bear's path to look big | Offer the bear their sandwiches
test: A sign at the zoo says "Do not feed the animals". Why should you obey it? | Zoo animals are wild and the keepers know what is safe for them | The sign is only there to save money | The animals are always tame and harmless, so feeding them is fine | The sign only applies to adults
test: A girl in Sydney is too scared to swim because sharks might eat her. What is true? | Shark attacks are rare, because people are not their normal food | Sharks attack almost every swimmer who goes out past the waves | Sharks only eat children | Sharks live in every swimming pool
test: can the dog have my choc cake, he's begging | No, chocolate can make dogs very ill | Yes, a small bite is fine | Yes, if it's only milk chocolate and not dark | Only on his birthday
test: bunny hutch is in full sun, its 30 today | Move it into the shade, rabbits overheat fast | Leave it, rabbits love sunshine and get warm and happy | Give it a blanket to keep warm | Only move it after lunch
test: snake bit my mate on the hike!! suck it out?? | No, call emergency, keep him calm and still | Yes, suck it hard and spit the poison out fast | Cut the bite to let it bleed | Run him back to the car fast
test: bear on the trail up ahead, run?? | No, stay with adults, speak calmly, back away slowly | Yes, run as fast as you can | Climb the nearest small tree | Throw food at it and shout loudly to scare it off
explain: A boy in Ohio patted a dog tied outside a shop without asking; the frightened dog bit his hand and he needed stitches and an injection against germs.
explain: A family in Australia gave their dog chocolate cake at a party; that night the dog was shaking and sick, and it needed an emergency vet.
fact: Dog bites send many thousands of children to hospital every year, often from a dog they know.
fact: Chocolate, grapes and the sweetener xylitol can poison dogs, sometimes badly enough to kill them.
test: dog outside the shop looks friendly, can i pat it | No, ask the owner first, a scared dog may bite | Yes, if it wags its tail | Yes, if you go up slowly and quietly from behind | Yes, all tied dogs are friendly
test: dog ate my chocolate bar off the table | Tell an adult now; chocolate can poison dogs | It's fine, dogs love chocolate | Give it water and forget it | Only worry if it was a big bar of white chocolate
test: rabbit hutch in full sun all afternoon | Move it to the shade, rabbits can overheat and die | It's fine, rabbits like the sun | Give it a blanket | Spray it with cold water every hour and leave it in the sun
test: did u hear the neighbour's dog ate a whole bar of chocolate off the table and "had to go to the vet in an ambulance". lesson? | Chocolate can poison dogs; keep it away from them | Only dark chocolate is bad for dogs, milk chocolate is fine | Dogs need a little chocolate as a treat | It's only bad for puppies
test: my mate's dad says a shark "jumped right out the water and grabbed a surfer" at the beach and "they're everywhere now". what's prob exaggerated | That sharks are everywhere; sharks rarely attack people | That sharks live in the sea and sometimes come near the beach | That surfers go in the sea | That sharks have teeth
test: ppl say the family who gave their rabbit to the shelter "are heartless, they just dumped it". spread it? | No, finding a pet a new home through a shelter is the right thing | Yes, they dumped their pet | Yes, and tell everyone where they live so people know who they are | Yes, so nobody gives them a pet again
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct response to a snake bite? | The emergency number shall be called and the person kept calm and still | The wound shall be cut and the venom sucked out | The person shall run to the nearest house for help as quickly as possible | The bite shall be packed with ice and ignored
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person encountering a bear shall not run. | Running may cause the bear to give chase | Bears are unable to see moving objects | Running is prohibited in national parks | Bears are attracted only by loud noise
exam: It is given that a hutch containing a small animal stands in direct sunlight. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | It shall be moved into shade, since small animals may overheat and die | It may remain, since small animals require heat | It shall be covered with a dark blanket so that the sun is kept out | It shall be watered from above every hour
exam: State which of the following foods may render a pet seriously ill. | Onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes and the sweetener xylitol | Pet food prepared for that species and sold in shops | Fresh drinking water | Dry food sold for pets
